{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Childhood acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes a large number of abnormal blood cells.","source_span":"Childhood acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer in which the bone marrow makes a large number of abnormal blood cells."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia.","source_span":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"In AML, myeloid stem cells usually become abnormal immature white blood cells called myeloblasts (myeloid blasts).","source_span":"In AML, the myeloid stem cells usually become a type of immature white blood cell called myeloblasts (or myeloid blasts)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Leukemia cells in AML can spread outside the blood to the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord), skin, and gums.","source_span":"The leukemia cells can spread outside the blood to other parts of the body, including the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord), skin, and gums."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"A solid tumor formed by AML leukemia cells is called a granulocytic sarcoma or chloroma.","source_span":"Sometimes leukemia cells form a solid tumor called a granulocytic sarcoma or chloroma."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Treatment of AML differs when it is the subtype acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) or when the child has Down syndrome.","source_span":"The treatment of AML is different when it is a subtype called acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) or when the child has Down syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Chronic myelogenous leukemia (CML) is rare in children.","source_span":"CML is rare in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML) is a rare childhood cancer that occurs more often in children around age 2 and is more common in boys.","source_span":"Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML) is a rare childhood cancer that occurs more often in children around the age of 2 years and is more common in boys."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"In JMML, too many bone marrow stem cells become myelocytes and monocytes, two types of white blood cells.","source_span":"In JMML, too many bone marrow stem cells become 2 types of white blood cells called myelocytes and monocytes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) occur less often in children than in adults, and over time MDS may become AML.","source_span":"Myelodysplastic syndromes (MDS) occur less often in children than in adults... Over time, MDS may become AML."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Transient myeloproliferative disorder (TMD), a type of MDS that can develop in newborns with Down syndrome, usually goes away on its own within the first 3 weeks of life.","source_span":"This disorder of the bone marrow can develop in newborns who have Down syndrome. It usually goes away on its own within the first 3 weeks of life."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Infants who have Down syndrome and TMD have an increased chance of developing AML before the age of 3 years.","source_span":"Infants who have Down syndrome and TMD have an increased chance of developing AML before the age of 3 years."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"Therapy-related AML (t-AML) and therapy-related MDS (t-MDS) usually occur within 7 years after treatment, but are rare in children.","source_span":"These therapy-related diseases usually occur within 7 years after treatment, but are rare in children."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","statement":"The risk of therapy-related myeloid diseases depends on the total dose of anticancer drugs used and the radiation dose and treatment field.","source_span":"The risk of these therapy-related myeloid diseases depends on the total dose of the anticancer drugs used and the radiation dose and treatment field."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What are some other names for childhood acute myeloid leukemia (AML)?","answer":"AML is also called acute myelogenous leukemia, acute myeloblastic leukemia, acute granulocytic leukemia, and acute nonlymphocytic leukemia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What is a solid tumor formed by AML leukemia cells called?","answer":"It is called a granulocytic sarcoma or chloroma."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[7],"question":"At what age does juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML) occur most often, and which sex is more commonly affected?","answer":"JMML occurs more often in children around the age of 2 years and is more common in boys."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[10],"question":"How long does transient myeloproliferative disorder (TMD) usually take to resolve in newborns with Down syndrome?","answer":"TMD usually goes away on its own within the first 3 weeks of life."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What increased risk do infants with Down syndrome and TMD face?","answer":"They have an increased chance of developing AML before the age of 3 years."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[12,13],"question":"Within how many years after cancer treatment do therapy-related AML and MDS usually occur, and what determines the risk?","answer":"Therapy-related AML and MDS usually occur within 7 years after treatment (rare in children), and the risk depends on the total dose of anticancer drugs used and the radiation dose and treatment field."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000001_7","fact_refs":[5],"question":"When does treatment of childhood AML differ from the standard approach?","answer":"Treatment differs when the AML subtype is acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL) or when the child has Down syndrome."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"Adult soft tissue sarcoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the soft tissues of the body.","source_span":"Adult soft tissue sarcoma is a disease in which malignant (cancer) cells form in the soft tissues of the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"Adult soft tissue sarcomas can form almost anywhere in the body but are most common in the head, neck, arms, legs, trunk, and abdomen.","source_span":"Adult soft tissue sarcomas can form almost anywhere in the body, but are most common in the head, neck, arms, legs, trunk, and abdomen."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"Inherited disorders that increase the risk of adult soft tissue sarcoma include retinoblastoma, neurofibromatosis type 1, tuberous sclerosis, familial adenomatous polyposis, Li-Fraumeni syndrome, Werner syndrome, and nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome (Gorlin syndrome).","source_span":"Risk factors for soft tissue sarcoma include the following inherited disorders: Retinoblastoma. Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1; von Recklinghausen disease). Tuberous sclerosis (Bourneville disease). Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP; Gardner syndrome). Li-Fraumeni syndrome. Werner syndrome (adult progeria). Nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome (Gorlin syndrome)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"Other risk factors for soft tissue sarcoma include past radiation therapy, exposure to chemicals such as Thorotrast, vinyl chloride, or arsenic, and long-term lymphedema in the arms or legs.","source_span":"Other risk factors for soft tissue sarcoma include the following: Past treatment with radiation therapy for certain cancers. Being exposed to certain chemicals, such as Thorotrast (thorium dioxide), vinyl chloride, or arsenic. Having swelling (lymphedema) in the arms or legs for a long time."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"A sarcoma may appear as a painless lump under the skin, often on an arm or a leg.","source_span":"A sarcoma may appear as a painless lump under the skin, often on an arm or a leg."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"There are three types of biopsy that may be used to diagnose adult soft tissue sarcoma: incisional biopsy, core biopsy, and excisional biopsy.","source_span":"There are three types of biopsy that may be used: Incisional biopsy... Core biopsy... Excisional biopsy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"High-grade soft tissue sarcoma tumors usually grow and spread more quickly than low-grade tumors.","source_span":"High-grade tumors usually grow and spread more quickly than low-grade tumors."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","statement":"Treatment options and prognosis for adult soft tissue sarcoma depend on factors including tumor type, size/grade/stage, location, whether all tumor is surgically removed, patient age and health, and recurrence.","source_span":"The treatment options and prognosis (chance of recovery) depend on the following: The type of soft tissue sarcoma. The size, grade, and stage of the tumor. How fast the cancer cells are growing and dividing. Where the tumor is in the body. Whether all of the tumor is removed by surgery. The patient's age and general health. Whether the cancer has recurred (come back)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[1],"question":"In which parts of the body are adult soft tissue sarcomas most commonly found?","answer":"They are most common in the head, neck, arms, legs, trunk, and abdomen, though they can form almost anywhere in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which inherited disorders are risk factors for adult soft tissue sarcoma?","answer":"Risk factors include retinoblastoma, neurofibromatosis type 1, tuberous sclerosis, familial adenomatous polyposis, Li-Fraumeni syndrome, Werner syndrome, and nevoid basal cell carcinoma syndrome (Gorlin syndrome)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What chemical exposures are listed as risk factors for soft tissue sarcoma?","answer":"Exposure to Thorotrast (thorium dioxide), vinyl chloride, or arsenic are listed as risk factors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What are the three types of biopsy that may be used to diagnose adult soft tissue sarcoma?","answer":"Incisional biopsy, core biopsy, and excisional biopsy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How does the growth rate of high-grade soft tissue sarcoma tumors compare to low-grade tumors?","answer":"High-grade tumors usually grow and spread more quickly than low-grade tumors."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_1","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What does a soft tissue sarcoma often look like as it first appears?","answer":"It may appear as a painless lump under the skin, often on an arm or a leg."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor (GIST) is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the gastrointestinal tract.","source_span":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor is a disease in which abnormal cells form in the tissues of the gastrointestinal tract."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"GISTs are most common in the stomach and small intestine but may be found anywhere in or near the GI tract.","source_span":"They are most common in the stomach and small intestine but may be found anywhere in or near the GI tract."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Some scientists believe GISTs begin in cells called interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC) in the wall of the GI tract.","source_span":"Some scientists believe that GISTs begin in cells called interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC), in the wall of the GI tract."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Genetic syndromes linked to GIST include neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) and Carney triad.","source_span":"The following genetic syndromes have been linked to GIST: Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1). Carney triad."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Signs of GIST include blood in the stool or vomit, abdominal pain, feeling very tired, trouble or pain when swallowing, and feeling full after eating only a little food.","source_span":"Blood (either bright red or very dark) in the stool or vomit. Pain in the abdomen, which may be severe. Feeling very tired. Trouble or pain when swallowing. Feeling full after only a little food is eaten."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Some GISTs are smaller than the eraser on top of a pencil and very small GISTs are common.","source_span":"Sometimes GISTs are smaller than the eraser on top of a pencil."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"Cancer spreads through the body via tissue, the lymph system, and the blood.","source_span":"Cancer can spread through tissue, the lymph system, and the blood"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","statement":"A PET scan detects malignant tumor cells by injecting a small amount of radioactive glucose into a vein and imaging where the glucose is used.","source_span":"A small amount of radioactive glucose (sugar) is injected into a vein. The PET scanner rotates around the body and makes a picture of where glucose is being used in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[1],"question":"In which parts of the gastrointestinal tract are GISTs most commonly found?","answer":"GISTs are most common in the stomach and small intestine, though they may be found anywhere in or near the GI tract."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What cell type do some scientists believe gastrointestinal stromal tumors originate from?","answer":"Some scientists believe GISTs begin in interstitial cells of Cajal (ICC) in the wall of the GI tract."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Which genetic syndromes have been linked to gastrointestinal stromal tumors?","answer":"Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) and Carney triad have been linked to GIST."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the signs and symptoms of a gastrointestinal stromal tumor?","answer":"Signs include blood in the stool or vomit, abdominal pain, feeling very tired, trouble or pain when swallowing, and feeling full after eating only a little food."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[5],"question":"How small can gastrointestinal stromal tumors sometimes be?","answer":"Some GISTs are smaller than the eraser on top of a pencil, and such very small tumors are common."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000003_2","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What are the three ways cancer can spread through the body, according to the document?","answer":"Cancer can spread through tissue, the lymph system, and the blood."}
